description:
Write the core of lesson 6.01's level editor: the map, the four tools, undo and redo, and the file format. The ImGui part on top of it (palette, viewport, menus) is then a thin layer that calls these functions.

```cpp
class Level
{
public:
    Level(int width, int height, char fill);
    int width() const;
    int height() const;
    char at(int x, int y) const;                  // ' ' outside the map

    void strokeBegin();                           // mouse pressed with the paint or erase tool
    void strokePaint(int x, int y, char tile);    // every frame while held: set one tile (outside the map: ignored)
    void strokeEnd();                             // mouse released: one undo step for the whole stroke
    void rectangle(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, char tile);   // corners in any order, both included, one step
    void floodFill(int x, int y, char tile);      // the connected region (4 directions) of that tile's kind, one step

    bool undo();
    bool redo();
    bool dirty() const;
    void markSaved();

    std::string save() const;
    static bool load(const std::string& text, Level& out);
};
```

**Steps.** Each stroke, rectangle or fill is **one** undo step holding, for every tile it changed, the old and the new character. A step that changes nothing is not recorded. A new step clears redo; `dirty` works like lesson 5.04's practice (a new level is not dirty).

**Rectangles and fills** clip to the map. A flood fill on a tile that already has the new kind does nothing. Use an explicit stack or queue for the fill, not recursion.

**The file format** (`save`): lines separated by `\n`:

```
xenon-level 1
size <width> <height>
tiles
<height lines of exactly width characters>
```

`load` accepts exactly this (lines may end with `\r\n`), with version `1` only, a width and height from 1 to 1000, and the right number of rows of the right length; otherwise it returns `false` and leaves `out` unchanged. A loaded level has empty undo history and is not dirty.

